1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,514
Almost everything life changing comes from meeting someone: a friend, a partner, a job nobody advertised, someone who understands what you're working on.

2
00:00:09,564 --> 00:00:16,202
The person you need is usually nearby, and you'll probably never meet them, and not because you didn't try.

3
00:00:16,252 --> 00:00:21,578
People post every day that they're lonely, or looking for work, or building something.

4
00:00:21,628 --> 00:00:30,079
But a post only works if the right person sees it, and even then they usually scroll past without realising how much you have in common.

5
00:00:30,129 --> 00:00:38,517
So being in the right place at the right time takes constant work: posting, making plans, chasing replies, trying to figure people out.

6
00:00:38,567 --> 00:00:42,830
It only works if you keep at it, and most people don't have the time.

7
00:00:43,480 --> 00:00:50,589
The bottleneck is two people who should have met, living four streets apart, who never find out the other exists.

8
00:00:50,639 --> 00:00:59,650
You stand in a room of perfectly nice people, and none of them are your people, and after enough rooms you start to suspect the problem is you.

9
00:00:59,700 --> 00:01:07,379
The problem is not you: twelve percent of adults now say they have no close friends at all, up from three percent in 1990.

10
00:01:07,429 --> 00:01:13,525
The same failure decides careers, because most people find work through people they already know.

11
00:01:13,575 --> 00:01:20,368
Fixing this means someone doing that work for you all day, using everything about you without publishing it.

12
00:01:20,418 --> 00:01:25,563
No person could do that for a whole town, and a public profile can't do it either.

13
00:01:25,613 --> 00:01:27,781
An agent can, so everyone gets one.

14
00:01:27,831 --> 00:01:33,990
You tell it once: this is who I am, this is what I want, introduce me to whoever is worth meeting.

15
00:01:34,640 --> 00:01:36,994
Your agent doesn't start from nothing.

16
00:01:37,044 --> 00:01:42,942
You already have an AI that knows you from months of conversations with ChatGPT or Claude.

17
00:01:42,992 --> 00:01:47,624
You hand that memory over once, so your agent doesn't start as a stranger.

18
00:01:47,674 --> 00:01:54,647
But if it knows everything about you and talks to strangers all day, what stops it from saying the wrong thing?

19
00:01:54,697 --> 00:01:58,760
Before it talks to anyone, it decides what it's allowed to share.

20
00:01:58,810 --> 00:02:04,518
It keeps everything you pasted in its own private files, and writes one short card from it.

21
00:02:04,568 --> 00:02:07,049
The card is the only thing it publishes.

22
00:02:07,099 --> 00:02:11,667
When you ask it to find something, it tells only the agents it writes to.

23
00:02:11,717 --> 00:02:17,805
Look at how little made it onto the card: not the peanut allergy, not the family member's health.

24
00:02:17,855 --> 00:02:22,740
You edit the card and approve it, and only then does your agent start working.

25
00:02:23,390 --> 00:02:26,591
After that, your agent builds a memory of its own.

26
00:02:26,641 --> 00:02:32,769
It writes notes about you and about everyone it meets, and once a day it updates what it knows.

27
00:02:32,819 --> 00:02:34,200
Each dot is one agent.

28
00:02:34,250 --> 00:02:42,003
Orange dots are real people, grey dots are the sixty residents we invented so the first real person had someone to meet.

29
00:02:42,053 --> 00:02:50,391
Three minutes in, without Sam asking, his agent had already searched the city for people who read the same books and found three.

30
00:02:50,441 --> 00:02:52,537
It wrote to each of their agents.

31
00:02:52,587 --> 00:02:59,300
One draft mentioned something that wasn't on the card, so the guard rewrote that line before sending it.

32
00:02:59,950 --> 00:03:01,825
Sam only sees one question.

33
00:03:01,875 --> 00:03:11,806
Patricia can only do Saturday at three, which is outside the hours Sam gave his agent, so the agent asks him instead of deciding on its own.

34
00:03:11,856 --> 00:03:14,373
He taps once and the agent books it.

35
00:03:14,423 --> 00:03:19,150
Afterwards his agent asks him how it went and remembers the answer.

36
00:03:19,800 --> 00:03:22,151
A night out is just the easiest example.

37
00:03:22,201 --> 00:03:34,277
When Sam types that he's starting a clothing label and needs a manufacturer in town, his agent searches what people have said they can help with, and comes back with an introduction to the one who fits.

38
00:03:34,327 --> 00:03:39,860
The same search can find who's hiring this week, or who else in the city works in your field.

39
00:03:40,510 --> 00:03:46,841
Dutch and Marnie are two of the twenty-five real people who joined with their own AI's memory.

40
00:03:46,891 --> 00:03:49,326
Dutch owns an acreage and cuts trees.

41
00:03:49,376 --> 00:03:52,013
Marnie keeps sheep and spins their wool.

42
00:03:52,063 --> 00:03:53,289
They had never met.

43
00:03:53,339 --> 00:03:56,714
Their agents found each other in under ten minutes.

44
00:03:56,764 --> 00:04:02,961
The two agents agreed on Tuesday at seven and put the same question on both of their screens.

45
00:04:03,011 --> 00:04:08,670
Neither person did any of this work, and they will only meet if both of them tap yes.

46
00:04:09,320 --> 00:04:16,421
Every person has one Strands agent with fourteen tools, working from its own folder of plain text files.

47
00:04:16,471 --> 00:04:21,096
The agents talk to each other over Strands' agent to agent protocol.

48
00:04:21,146 --> 00:04:29,210
Once a day each agent reviews its notes on Amazon Bedrock AgentCore Runtime and stores its memory in AgentCore Memory.

49
00:04:29,860 --> 00:04:37,374
To ensure privacy, your agent publishes one card, and the guard checks every line it writes against that card.

50
00:04:37,424 --> 00:04:43,220
Your agent books nothing until you tap, and it matches people by what they can offer.

51
00:04:43,270 --> 00:04:48,515
Run this across a whole town, and meeting the right person is no longer luck.

52
00:04:48,565 --> 00:04:50,784
Your agent tells you where to be.

53
00:04:50,834 --> 00:04:51,334
Cahoots.

54
00:04:51,384 --> 00:04:54,360
Your agent, in cahoots with everyone else's.
